{
  "summary": "The previous proposal correctly identifies the daemon thread as a root cause and the lack of audit trail as a gap, but has several weaknesses. First, changing daemon=True to daemon=False is risky in the TUI context \u2014 if the script hangs or takes a long time, it will block the entire TUI process from exiting cleanly, potentially leaving orphan terminal state. A better approach would be to keep the daemon thread but use atexit hooks or signal handlers that join with a timeout, or better yet, use subprocess.Popen to launch the script as a detached child process so it survives independent of the Python process. Second, the proposal suggests writing history entries from within the background thread, but FeatureFile._save() does non-atomic JSON read-modify-write with no file locking \u2014 if the main thread also modifies the feature file (e.g., the pipeline moves on to the next item), the background thread's write could clobber those changes or vice versa. The audit trail writes need either file-level locking or a separate append-only log mechanism. Third, the proposal doesn't address the fact that execute_done_script receives no reference to whether the FeatureFile has already been moved to the 'done' directory \u2014 if the file path changes between trigger and completion callback, the history write will fail silently. Finally, the proposal omits timeout handling: if a done_script never completes, there's no mechanism to detect this, mark it as timed out, or alert the user."
